\chapter{Debugging Tangled Code}
\label{ch:debug}

Chapter~\ref{ch:languages} explained \emph{what} section markers are and
how weft produces them. This chapter is about \emph{using} them --- how a
marker turns an error in generated code into a precise location in your
\verb|.weft| source, for you, for the compiler, and for tools.

\section{The four-step workflow}

Tracing any error back to its source is the same short procedure:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Locate} the failing line in the tangled file --- e.g.\
\verb|server.js:47| from a stack trace.
\item \textbf{Read upward} to the nearest opening marker,
\verb|// {3: routes.weft:42}|, which names the scrap and its source.
\item \textbf{Open} \verb|routes.weft:42|. The scrap is there, wrapped in
the prose that explains its intent --- which often makes the bug obvious.
\item \textbf{Fix, re-tangle, done.} Never edit the tangled file; the
markers are there to remind you it is generated.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Nested markers point most precisely.} When fragments invoke
fragments, markers nest like parentheses. Read the \emph{innermost}
enclosing pair --- it names the most specific source location:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
// {3: routes.weft:42}
app.get("/api/users/:id", async (req, res) => {
  // {7: database.weft:18}
  const user = await db.query("SELECT ... WHERE id=$1", [req.params.id]);
  // {:7}
  res.json(user);
});
// {:3}
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent If the bug is inside the \verb|{7:...{:7}| block, go to
\verb|database.weft:18|, not \verb|routes.weft:42|.

\paragraph{Let the compiler do it.} For C-family languages the
\verb|#line| directive weft emits makes the compiler report against the
source directly:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
parser.weft:84: error: 'token_type' undeclared
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent instead of pointing at \verb|parser.c|. Go straight to
\verb|parser.weft:84|; the prose around the scrap says what
\verb|token_type| is and where it is defined.

\section{Common situations}

\begin{description}
\item[Runtime error with a stack trace.] Open the file and line it names,
read up to the nearest marker, open that \verb|.weft| location.
\item[Wrong output, no error.] Search the tangled file for the suspect
code; its marker tells you which scrap to examine. Often the prose says
one thing (``triple each value'') and the code does another (\verb|* 2|)
--- bug found.
\item[Which definition produced this line?] Fragments defined in several
pieces accumulate, but each piece has its own scrap number and source
line, so the marker disambiguates them.
\end{description}

\paragraph{Handy habits.}
\begin{itemize}
\item \verb|grep -n '{[0-9]*: database.weft:' server.js| lists every scrap
that came from one source file.
\item A closing \verb|{:7}| with no visible \verb|{7:| above means the
scrap is taller than your screen --- scroll up.
\item If your test runner prints code on failure, the markers point
straight at the literate source.
\end{itemize}

\section{Doing it automatically}

You rarely need to trace by hand. weft reads the markers for you:
\verb|weft -R server.js:47| returns the source location as JSON, and
piping a build log through \verb|weft --errors| annotates every
\textit{file:line} it recognises with its \verb|.weft| origin. Both work
on the tangled output alone --- no \verb|.weft| sources needed --- which
is what makes them ideal in CI and for AI agents. See
Chapter~\ref{ch:ai}.
